## Two outcomes, not three

There is no Ruby backend yet — that is the point of running the audit before the port. A gap found now is a gap the port never has.

| Result on the native backend | Meaning | Action |
|---|---|---|
| behaves as you predicted from the Rust | the corpus got stronger | keep the case — a standalone win for the corpus regardless of #<%= parent %> |
| behaves differently | you misread the Rust, or it is an upstream bug the corpus was hiding | work out which. If it is real, open a `<%= divergence_label %>` issue **on this repo** (see below). **Never pin surprising behaviour as golden silently** |

The "fails on Ruby → port gap" outcome is gone, because there is no Ruby backend to fail. Preventing that outcome is strictly better than diagnosing it.
